=====================================================================
PART 1: CPU CACHE AND CACHE LINES
=====================================================================

Q1. What is a cache line? What is its size?
-------------------------------------------
A cache line is the smallest unit of data that moves between main
memory and the CPU caches. The CPU never fetches a single byte; it
fetches a whole block. On most x86 and ARM server CPUs that block is
64 bytes. (Some chips differ, for example Apple M-series uses 128
bytes in parts of the hierarchy.)

Why it matters: when you read one value, the neighbouring bytes come
along for free. That gives us spatial locality, which is why
sequential array access is fast. The flip side is false sharing.

Yaad rakho: cache line = 64 BYTES (bits nahi). Ek line mein 16 ints
ya 8 longs.

Senior line: "Memory access cost is dominated by cache misses, so data
layout often matters more than instruction count."


Q2. What is false sharing? How do you detect and fix it?
--------------------------------------------------------
False sharing happens when two threads on different cores write to
DIFFERENT variables that happen to sit on the SAME cache line. The
variables are logically independent, but the hardware tracks
ownership per line, not per variable. Each write forces the other
core's copy of the line to be invalidated, so the line bounces
between cores. Throughput can drop several times.

Detection:
 - Symptom: multi-threaded code does not scale with cores even
   though threads touch separate data.
 - Benchmark with JMH, with and without padding, and compare.
 - On Linux, "perf c2c" reports cache lines with contended access.
   Intel VTune also has memory-access analysis.

Fixes:
 - Padding: put unused fields between the hot variables so they land
   on separate lines.
 - @Contended (jdk.internal.vm.annotation.Contended). It pads
   fields (128 bytes by default). Outside the JDK you need
   -XX:-RestrictContended to enable it.
 - Redesign: per-thread state, striping (what LongAdder does).

Code:
   class PaddedCounters {
       volatile long a;
       long p1, p2, p3, p4, p5, p6, p7;   // 56 bytes padding
       volatile long b;
   }
Caveat: the JVM may reorder or strip fields, so @Contended is more
reliable than hand-written padding.

Senior line: "I would first prove it with a benchmark or perf c2c,
because padding wastes memory and should only go on proven hot spots."


Q3. Why is LongAdder faster than AtomicLong under high contention?
------------------------------------------------------------------
AtomicLong has one shared value. Every increment is a CAS on that
single memory location, so under heavy contention threads keep
failing CAS and retrying, and the cache line ping-pongs between
cores.

LongAdder (built on Striped64) keeps a base value plus an array of
Cells. Contended threads hash to different cells and update them
independently, and the cells are padded with @Contended so they do
not false-share. sum() adds base plus all cells.

Trade-off: sum() is not an atomic snapshot, so LongAdder is great
for statistics and counters, but not when you need an exact value at
a precise moment (for example, sequence generation or
compare-and-set logic).

Yaad rakho: ek jagah ka bheed -> kai cells mein bant diya, aur
cells ko alag cache lines di.


Q4. Which is faster to sum, int[] or Integer[], and why?
---------------------------------------------------------
int[] is faster, mainly because of cache behaviour.
 - int[] stores values contiguously. One 64-byte cache line holds 16
   ints, so after the first miss the next 15 reads are cache hits
   (spatial locality), and the hardware prefetcher also helps.
 - Integer[] stores only references contiguously. Each Integer
   object lives somewhere on the heap, so every element needs an
   extra pointer dereference ("pointer chasing") that is often a
   cache miss going to RAM (about 100 ns vs about 1 ns for L1).
 - Memory overhead: an Integer object has a header (about 12-16
   bytes) plus the value, so a line holds far fewer values and more
   lines must be fetched. There is also unboxing cost.

Senior line: "That is why performance-critical code uses primitive
arrays or primitive-specialised collections like fastutil or
Eclipse Collections."

Interviewer is checking: do you understand that performance depends
on memory layout, not only algorithmic complexity.


