PART 2: CACHE COHERENCY (MESI)
=====================================================================

Q5. Explain the four MESI states.
---------------------------------
MESI is a cache coherence protocol. Each cache line in each core's
cache is in one of four states:
 - Modified: this core has the only valid copy and it differs from
   RAM (dirty). Memory is stale.
 - Exclusive: this core has the only copy and it matches RAM (clean).
 - Shared: several cores may hold the line; all copies match RAM.
 - Invalid: this core's copy is stale or absent and must be
   re-fetched before use.

Yaad rakho: M = maine likha, E = akela owner, S = sab padh rahe,
I = copy bekaar.


Q6. Core 2 writes a line that is Shared with Core 1. What happens?
-------------------------------------------------------------------
Core 2 must first get exclusive ownership. It broadcasts an
invalidate (an "upgrade" or RFO, read-for-ownership, if it did not
have the line). Core 1 snoops the message and marks its copy
Invalid. Core 2's copy becomes Modified and it writes. If Core 1
later reads, it gets a miss; Core 2 supplies the data (and memory is
updated), and both end up Shared.

This is write-invalidate: reading shared data is cheap, writing
shared data is expensive.


Q7. Explain false sharing using MESI.
-------------------------------------
Variables a and b share one line. Core 1 writes a: its line goes to
Modified, Core 2's goes Invalid. Core 2 writes b: it must take the
line, Core 1's goes Invalid, Core 2's goes Modified. Repeat. The
line ping-pongs M <-> I between cores, and every write costs a
cache miss plus bus traffic even though there is no real data
sharing.


Q8. Core 1 holds a line in Exclusive and writes it. Is a bus message
sent?
-------------------------------------------------------------------
No. Exclusive means no other core has a copy, so there is nobody to
invalidate. The core transitions E -> M silently. That is the whole
point of the E state: it makes private writes cheap. A message only
flows when another core reads (snooping): the owner supplies the
data and both go to Shared.

Term to use: snooping, each cache watches the bus for requests
about lines it holds.


Q9. Hardware is cache coherent, so why do we still need volatile?
-----------------------------------------------------------------
This is the most asked question in this area. Coherence and
visibility ordering are different things.

 - Coherence only guarantees that all cores eventually agree on the
   value of a SINGLE memory location. It says nothing about the
   ORDER in which writes to DIFFERENT locations become visible.
 - Store buffers: a core puts a write in a store buffer first; it
   reaches the cache later, so other cores see it late. Invalidate
   queues delay the reader side too.
 - CPU out-of-order execution can reorder independent instructions.
 - The compiler/JIT can reorder, and can keep a variable in a
   register or hoist a read out of a loop. Hardware coherence cannot
   help here because the value never even reaches the cache.

volatile fixes all of these: the JIT cannot cache or hoist it, and
the barriers it emits stop reordering and flush stores.

Senior line: "Coherence is about one address; the memory model is
about ordering across addresses. volatile gives me the second."


Q10. Write-invalidate vs write-update?
--------------------------------------
Write-invalidate: on a write, other copies are marked Invalid and
re-fetched only if needed. Low bus traffic when the same core writes
repeatedly. This is what modern CPUs use.
Write-update: on a write, the new value is broadcast to all holders.
Readers always have fresh data, but every write generates traffic,
even for lines nobody will read again.


=====================================================================
PART 3: INSTRUCTION REORDERING
=====================================================================

Q11. What is instruction reordering, and who does it?
-----------------------------------------------------
The order in which operations actually execute or become visible can
differ from the order in the source code. Three layers can do it:
 1. Compiler / JIT: reorders statements, eliminates redundant reads,
    keeps values in registers, hoists loads out of loops.
 2. CPU out-of-order execution: runs whichever instructions are
    ready, to avoid stalling.
 3. Memory system: store buffers and caches make a write visible to
    other cores later than a subsequent read or write.
It is allowed because it improves performance and, for a single
thread, nobody can tell.


Q12. What is as-if-serial semantics? Why is reordering safe in single
thread but not in multi-thread?
---------------------------------------------------------------------
As-if-serial: the compiler and CPU may reorder anything as long as
the result observable within a single thread is the same as
sequential execution. Independent operations (no data dependency)
can be swapped.

In a single thread the program cannot observe the swap. In
multi-thread code, another thread CAN observe the intermediate
states. For example, "data = 42; ready = true;" has no dependency
between the two lines, so they may be swapped, and a reader that
sees ready == true might still see data == 0.

Classic litmus test:
   T1: x = 1; r1 = y;      T2: y = 1; r2 = x;
Without reordering, (r1, r2) = (0, 0) is impossible. With
reordering (each thread's load moves before its store, or the store
sits in the store buffer), (0, 0) is possible.


Q13. Why does a non-volatile flag loop sometimes never terminate?
-----------------------------------------------------------------
   boolean ready;   while (!ready) { }
Without volatile or synchronization, there is no happens-before
edge between the writer thread and this loop. The JIT is allowed to
assume no other thread changes ready, so it can read it once, keep
it in a register, and effectively compile the loop to
while (true). Even without the JIT, the reader may keep seeing a
stale cached value. Making the field volatile prevents hoisting and
creates the happens-before edge.

Practical: run it with and without volatile; the broken version
typically hangs after the JIT compiles the loop (often needs the
loop to be hot).


Q14. Why is volatile needed in Double-Checked Locking?
------------------------------------------------------
   inst = new Singleton();
is three steps: (1) allocate memory, (2) run the constructor,
(3) assign the reference to inst. Steps 2 and 3 can be reordered. A
second thread doing the unsynchronized first check "if (inst ==
null)" may see a non-null reference to a partially constructed
object and use it.

Declaring the field volatile makes the write of the reference a
release: everything done in the constructor happens-before any
thread that reads the non-null reference. So it is about ORDERING
(safe publication), not only visibility.

Alternatives I prefer: the initialization-on-demand holder idiom
(a static nested class, relies on class-init guarantees), or an
enum singleton.


=====================================================================
PART 4: MEMORY BARRIERS
=====================================================================

Q15. What is a memory barrier? What types exist?
------------------------------------------------
A memory barrier (fence) is an instruction or compiler directive
that prevents certain reorderings across it and forces the effects
of earlier operations to become visible before later ones.
Four conceptual types (first op, then second op):
 - LoadLoad: loads before the barrier complete before loads after.
 - LoadStore: loads before are ordered before stores after.
 - StoreStore: stores before are visible before stores after.
 - StoreLoad: stores before are visible before loads after.
A full fence combines all four.


Q16. Which barrier is the most expensive and why?
-------------------------------------------------
StoreLoad. To order an earlier store before a later load, the core
must drain its store buffer (make all pending writes globally
visible) while later loads wait. The other three are cheap or free
on strongly ordered hardware.


Q17. Which barriers are inserted for volatile reads and writes?
---------------------------------------------------------------
Conceptually (the conservative JSR-133 cookbook mapping):
 - Volatile write: StoreStore (and LoadStore) barrier BEFORE the
   write, StoreLoad barrier AFTER it. This is "release" behaviour:
   all earlier writes become visible no later than the volatile
   write.
 - Volatile read: LoadLoad and LoadStore barriers AFTER the read.
   This is "acquire" behaviour: later operations cannot move above
   the read.
The JIT may elide barriers it can prove unnecessary. On x86 only
the StoreLoad part needs a real instruction (HotSpot emits
lock addl $0,(%rsp) after the volatile store). On ARMv8 HotSpot
uses ldar/stlr (acquire/release instructions) instead of separate
barrier instructions.


Q18. What is the difference between x86 and ARM here? Does "works on
x86" mean the code is correct?
--------------------------------------------------------------------
x86 follows a strong model (Total Store Order): the only reordering
the hardware does is Store->Load (via the store buffer). ARM and
POWER have weak models: loads and stores can be reordered in many
more ways, so more barriers are needed.

So a data race can appear to work on x86 and fail on ARM (Apple
silicon, AWS Graviton). Also, the JIT can reorder regardless of
hardware. Correctness must come from the Java Memory Model rules
(volatile, synchronized, final, etc.), never from observed behaviour
on one CPU.


Q19. What happens to normal writes made before a volatile write?
----------------------------------------------------------------
They are visible to any thread that subsequently reads that volatile
variable and sees the written value. This works through
happens-before: program order (the normal write happens-before the
volatile write) plus the volatile rule (write happens-before the
later read) plus transitivity. It is called "piggybacking" on
volatile.

   int data; volatile boolean ready;
   T1: data = 42; ready = true;
   T2: if (ready) print(data);   // guaranteed 42

Note: making data volatile as well adds cost and no extra
correctness here.


=====================================================================
PART 5: JAVA MEMORY MODEL AND HAPPENS-BEFORE
=====================================================================

Q20. What is the Java Memory Model and why do we need it?
---------------------------------------------------------
The JMM (JLS chapter 17, JSR-133) is a specification that defines
what values a read may see and which reorderings are legal, for
every JVM on every hardware. It lets programmers reason about
concurrency without knowing CPU details. It covers three things:
 - Atomicity: an operation is indivisible (single reads/writes of
   most types, volatile, Atomic*, locks).
 - Visibility: when one thread's writes become visible to another.
 - Ordering: which instruction orders are guaranteed.
The central tool is the happens-before relation.


Q21. What is happens-before? List the main rules.
-------------------------------------------------
If action A happens-before action B, then the effects of A are
visible to B and A is ordered before B. It is a guarantee about
visibility and ordering, not a statement about wall-clock time.
Rules:
 1. Program order: within one thread, each action happens-before
    every later action in program order.
 2. Monitor lock: an unlock of monitor m happens-before every later
    lock of the same m.
 3. Volatile: a write to a volatile variable happens-before every
    later read of that same variable.
 4. Thread start: a call to Thread.start() happens-before any action
    in the started thread.
 5. Thread termination/join: all actions in a thread happen-before
    another thread successfully returns from join() on it (or sees
    isAlive() == false).
 6. Transitivity: if A hb B and B hb C, then A hb C.
Others: interrupt() happens-before the interrupted thread detects
it; default values (0/null) are written before any action in a
program; end of a constructor happens-before the start of the
finalizer. java.util.concurrent adds more (for example, actions
before submitting to an Executor, and before putting into a
concurrent collection, happen-before the consuming action).


Q22. How is happens-before different from "happened earlier in time"?
----------------------------------------------------------------------
Time order does not give visibility. If T1 writes at 10:00:00 and
T2 reads at 10:00:05 with no synchronization between them, there is
no happens-before edge, so T2 is NOT guaranteed to see the write.
Conversely, happens-before does not require actual temporal order of
execution; it is a logical guarantee. Visibility comes only from
edges built with synchronization actions.


Q23. What is a data race? How is it different from a race
condition?
----------------------------------------------------------
Data race (JLS definition): two accesses to the same variable, at
least one a write, not ordered by happens-before. The JMM gives very
weak guarantees for racy programs (stale values, and for non-volatile
long/double possibly torn values).

Race condition: a logic bug where correctness depends on the timing
of threads, for example check-then-act: "if (!map.containsKey(k))
map.put(k, v)" with a thread-safe map still races logically.

They are independent: you can have a race condition without a data
race (every access is synchronized but the compound logic is not
atomic) and a data race that happens to be harmless.


Q24. Two threads synchronize on different monitors. Is that safe?
-----------------------------------------------------------------
No. A happens-before edge exists only between an unlock and a later
lock of the SAME monitor object. Different locks give mutual
exclusion for neither and create no visibility edge. Also, if only
the writer synchronizes and the reader reads plainly, there is no
edge either: both sides must use the same lock (or volatile).

   Safe:    T1: synchronized(m){ x = 1; }  T2: synchronized(m){ r = x; }
   Unsafe:  T1: synchronized(m){ x = 1; }  T2: r = x;   // data race


Q25. What does the JMM guarantee for final fields?
--------------------------------------------------
If an object is constructed properly (the this reference does not
escape during construction), then any thread that obtains a
reference to it, even through a data race, is guaranteed to see the
correct values of its final fields, and of objects reachable only
through those final fields, as of the end of the constructor.
Non-final fields get no such guarantee without safe publication.
This is why immutable objects (all fields final) are inherently
thread-safe to share.
Pitfall: leaking this from a constructor (starting a thread,
registering a listener) breaks the guarantee.


Q26. What is safe publication? Ways to do it?
---------------------------------------------
Making an object reachable by other threads in a way that also
makes its state fully visible. Safe ways:
 - Initialize it in a static initializer.
 - Store the reference in a volatile field or AtomicReference.
 - Store it in a final field (of a properly constructed object).
 - Store it in a field guarded by a lock (and read it under the same
   lock).
 - Hand it over via a thread-safe collection or queue
   (ConcurrentHashMap, BlockingQueue) or an Executor.
Unsafe: assigning to a plain non-volatile field and reading it from
another thread.


Q27. How do Thread.start() and join() provide visibility?
----------------------------------------------------------
Everything the parent thread did before calling start()
happens-before everything the new thread does, so the child sees
all earlier writes without extra synchronization. Everything a
thread did happens-before the return of join() in the thread that
joined it, so after join() the joiner sees all of its writes. These
are two of the cheapest and most overlooked ways to share data
safely (for example, fork work, join, then read results).


=====================================================================
PART 6: VOLATILE IN DEPTH
=====================================================================

Q28. What does volatile guarantee, and what does it not?
--------------------------------------------------------
Guarantees:
 - Visibility: a read sees the latest write by any thread; the value
   is not cached in a register or stale cache.
 - Ordering: volatile accesses are not reordered with earlier or
   later memory operations in the ways the JMM forbids, and writes
   before a volatile write are visible after the matching read
   (happens-before).
 - Atomicity of the single read or write, including long and double
   (which may otherwise be split into two 32-bit halves).
Does NOT guarantee:
 - Atomicity of compound operations (i++, check-then-act,
   read-modify-write).
 - Mutual exclusion.
 - Anything about the contents behind a reference (see Q33).


Q29. Is "volatile int count; count++" thread-safe?
--------------------------------------------------
No. count++ is read, add, write: three steps. Two threads can both
read 0, both compute 1, both write 1, and one update is lost.
Visibility is fine, atomicity is missing. Fix: AtomicInteger
(incrementAndGet, which uses a CAS loop), LongAdder for heavy
contention, or synchronized/lock when it must be combined with other
state.


Q30. volatile vs synchronized vs AtomicInteger: when to use which?
------------------------------------------------------------------
 - volatile: one variable, written by one thread (or writes that do
   not depend on the old value), read by many. Flags, config
   references, safe publication.
 - Atomic* / LongAdder: lock-free atomic read-modify-write on a
   single variable (counters, CAS state machines).
 - synchronized / Lock: multiple variables that must change
   together (invariants), compound actions, or when you need mutual
   exclusion and blocking/waiting semantics.
Cost view: volatile read is cheap, volatile write has a StoreLoad
barrier, CAS costs a locked instruction and can retry under
contention, locks can block and context switch.


Q31. Is a plain (non-volatile) long read/write atomic?
------------------------------------------------------
The JLS (17.7) says a write to a non-volatile long or double may be
treated as two separate 32-bit writes, so another thread could see a
torn value (high half from one write, low half from another). In
practice, 64-bit HotSpot JVMs do 64-bit atomic writes, but the
specification does not guarantee it. Declare it volatile (or use
AtomicLong) when it is shared. All other primitives (and
references) are atomic for single reads/writes.


Q32. How does volatile create happens-before?
---------------------------------------------
A write to a volatile variable happens-before every subsequent read
of that same variable. Combined with program order and transitivity,
anything the writing thread did before the volatile write is visible
to a reading thread after it reads the volatile value. Mechanically
this is implemented with the barriers from Q17.


Q33. If I declare "volatile int[] arr", is arr[0] = 5 visible?
--------------------------------------------------------------
Not by itself. volatile applies to the reference variable arr, not
to the array elements. Writing the element is a plain write. Options:
use AtomicIntegerArray, VarHandle with volatile/acquire-release
access modes on elements, or synchronize. A common safe pattern is
to build a new array/immutable object and publish it by assigning
to the volatile reference (copy-on-write). Writes done BEFORE the
volatile assignment are visible after reading the reference.


Q34. Two threads run: if (!flag) { flag = true; doWork(); } with a
volatile boolean flag. Does doWork() run only once?
-------------------------------------------------------------------
No, it can run twice. This is a check-then-act race: both threads
can read flag == false before either writes true. volatile gives
visibility, not atomic check-and-set. Fix with
AtomicBoolean.compareAndSet(false, true), which does the check and
the set as one atomic step, or use a lock.


=====================================================================
RAPID-FIRE CHEAT SHEET (revise 10 minutes before the interview)
=====================================================================
 - Cache line = 64 bytes. Same line + different threads writing =
   false sharing. Fix: @Contended / padding / striping.
 - int[] fast, Integer[] slow: contiguous values vs pointer chasing.
 - MESI: M modified, E exclusive, S shared, I invalid. Write needs
   ownership, others go Invalid. E -> M is silent.
 - Coherence = one address agrees. Memory model = ordering across
   addresses. That is why volatile exists on coherent hardware.
 - Reordering by: compiler/JIT, CPU out-of-order, store buffer.
   Allowed if single-thread result is same (as-if-serial).
 - Barriers: LoadLoad, LoadStore, StoreStore, StoreLoad (costliest).
   Volatile write = release, volatile read = acquire.
 - x86 hides bugs (only Store->Load reorders). ARM exposes them.
 - JMM = rulebook. happens-before = visibility + ordering, not time.
 - HB rules: program order, monitor lock, volatile, start, join,
   transitivity.
 - Data race = conflicting access with no HB edge.
 - volatile = visibility + ordering + single read/write atomicity.
   NOT count++, NOT check-then-act.
 - DCL needs volatile because of reordering (publish before
   construct).
 - Same lock object on BOTH reader and writer, or no guarantee.

